<meta type=article>
<title | Bare boolean authoring forms (#219)>
<author | The Enscribe Team>
</meta>

<config toc number-sections />

A bare boolean attribute means `true`, the same as the `=true` / `+flag` forms — and it is the
canonical HTML / eHTML spelling. `<config toc number-sections>` ≡ `<config toc=true
number-sections=true>`. Only a *known* boolean promotes; a bare typo stays unrecognized.

<section | Introduction>

A numbered section (1).

<sub-section | Background>

A numbered sub-section (1.1).

<section unlisted | Colophon>

`<section unlisted>` (bare) ≡ `<section +unlisted>` — kept out of the contents listing, but still
**numbered** (2) and rendered. (`unlisted` is listing-only; it does not affect numbering.)

<section unnumbered | A Note on Method>

`<section unnumbered>` (bare) ≡ `<section +unnumbered>` — outside the numbered sequence: no number,
and the counter does not advance. (`unnumbered` is numbering-only; it is still *listed*.)

<section | Methods>

A numbered section (3) — Colophon took 2 (unlisted is still numbered); the unnumbered note did not
advance the counter.
